<subsection xml:id="subsec-curves">
  <title>Curves in <m>\R^n</m></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Up to now, integration has concerned itself with regions in the plane or in
      space.  This section and the next move from regions to <em>curves</em>:
      one-dimensional paths winding through two- or three-dimensional space.
      We begin by making the notion of a parametrized curve precise, and then
      develop the calculus tools — tangent vectors, velocities, and differentiation
      rules — that are needed to compute line integrals in
      <xref ref="subsec-scalar-line-integrals"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition: curve ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-curve">
    <title>Curve, Trace, Closed Curve, Differentiable Curve</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>I \subseteq \R</m> be an interval.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <term>curve parametrized over <m>I</m></term> is a function
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\colon I \to \R^n,
          \qquad
          t \mapsto
          \begin{pmatrix} x_1(t) \\ \vdots \\ x_n(t) \end{pmatrix},
        </me>
        whose component functions <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n \colon I \to \R</m> are
        continuous.
        The parameter <m>t</m> is often thought of as <em>time</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The set of all values taken by <m>\mathbf{x}</m>,
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}(I)
          = \left\{\, \begin{pmatrix} x_1(t) \\ \vdots \\ x_n(t) \end{pmatrix}
            \;\middle|\; t \in I \,\right\} \subseteq \R^n,
        </me>
        is called the <term>trace</term> of <m>\mathbf{x}</m>.
        The trace is the actual geometric path in space; the parametrization
        is one particular way of traversing that path.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>I = [a,b]</m> and <m>\mathbf{x}(a) = \mathbf{x}(b)</m>, the curve is
        <term>closed</term> (the path returns to its starting point).
        If the component functions <m>x_1, \ldots, x_n</m> are continuously
        differentiable, the curve is <term>continuously differentiable</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Example: helix ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-helix">
    <title>Helix</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Sketch the trace of the curve
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\colon [0, 4\pi] \to \R^3, \qquad
          t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} \cos t \\ \sin t \\ t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The first two components <m>(\cos t, \sin t)</m> trace the unit circle
        in the <m>xy</m>-plane, while the third component <m>t</m> increases
        monotonically from <m>0</m> to <m>4\pi</m>.
        The result is a <em>helix</em> that spirals upward around the
        <m>z</m>-axis, completing two full turns.
      </p>
      <p>
        Selected points help to orient the sketch:
      </p>
      <table xml:id="tab-helix-points">
        <caption>Sample points on the helix</caption>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor">
          <col right="minor"/> <col right="minor"/> <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><m>t</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\mathbf{x}(t)</m></cell>
            <cell>Position</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1,\,0,\,0)</m></cell>
            <cell>Start on the <m>x</m>-axis at height <m>0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\pi/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0,\,1,\,\pi/2)</m></cell>
            <cell>Quarter turn, height <m>\pi/2</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\pi</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-1,\,0,\,\pi)</m></cell>
            <cell>Half turn, height <m>\pi</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>2\pi</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1,\,0,\,2\pi)</m></cell>
            <cell>First full revolution at height <m>2\pi</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <figure xml:id="fig-helix">
        <caption>
          The helix <m>\mathbf{x}(t)=(\cos t,\sin t,t)</m> for
          <m>t \in [0,4\pi]</m>.  The projection onto the <m>xy</m>-plane is
          the unit circle; the height coordinate increases linearly with
          <m>t</m>, producing two full turns.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-helix" width="55%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[x={(0.80cm,-0.35cm)}, y={(0.80cm,0.35cm)}, z={(0cm,0.9cm)},
                                scale=0.85]
              % axes
              \draw[->,gray!60] (-1.6,0,0) -- (1.9,0,0) node[right,gray!60] {$x$};
              \draw[->,gray!60] (0,-1.6,0) -- (0,1.9,0) node[right,gray!60] {$y$};
              \draw[->,gray!60] (0,0,-0.3) -- (0,0,7.5) node[above,gray!60] {$z$};
              % xy-plane grid circle (projection)
              \draw[gray!40,dashed]
                plot[domain=0:360,samples=60,smooth]
                  ({cos(\x)},{sin(\x)},0);
              % helix: two full turns, t=0..4*pi
              \draw[blue!70!black, thick]
                plot[domain=0:720,samples=200,smooth]
                  ({cos(\x)},{sin(\x)},{0.0175*\x});
              % start and end markers
              \filldraw[blue!70!black] (1,0,0) circle (1.8pt) node[below right,font=\small] {$t=0$};
              \filldraw[blue!70!black] (1,0,{4*3.14159}) circle (1.8pt)
                node[above right,font=\small] {$t=4\pi$};
              % tick marks on z-axis
              \foreach \h/\lab in {3.14159/$\pi$, 6.28318/$2\pi$, 9.42478/$3\pi$, 12.56637/$4\pi$} {
                \draw[gray!60] (-0.07,0,\h) -- (0.07,0,\h);
                \node[left,gray!60,font=\tiny] at (0,0,\h) {\lab};
              }
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Example: intersection curve ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-cylinder-plane-intersection">
    <title>Intersection Curve of a Cylinder with a Plane</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine a parametrization of the curve of intersection of the
        cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> with the plane <m>y + z = 2</m>.
        That is, find an interval <m>I \subset \R</m> and a curve
        <m>\mathbf{x}\colon I \to \R^3</m> whose trace is the intersection.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The projection of the intersection onto the <m>xy</m>-plane is
        the unit circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, so we parametrize
        <me>
          x(t) = \cos t, \qquad y(t) = \sin t, \qquad t \in [0, 2\pi].
        </me>
        For the point <m>(\cos t, \sin t, z(t))</m> to lie in the plane
        <m>y + z = 2</m>, we need
        <me>
          z(t) = 2 - \sin t.
        </me>
        The parametrization is therefore
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\colon [0,2\pi] \to \R^3, \qquad
          t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} \cos t \\ \sin t \\ 2 - \sin t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        The trace is an ellipse tilted in space: at <m>y = 0</m> the height is
        <m>z = 2</m>, while at <m>y = 1</m> (the highest circle point) the
        height is <m>z = 1</m>, and at <m>y = -1</m> it is <m>z = 3</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Definition: tangent vector, velocity, regular ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-tangent-vector">
    <title>Tangent Vector, Velocity Vector, Speed, Regular Curve</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{x}\colon I \to \R^n</m> be a differentiable curve.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <term>tangent vector</term> of <m>\mathbf{x}</m> at time <m>t</m>
        is the component-wise derivative
        <me>
          \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)
          = \frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt}(t)
          = \begin{pmatrix} \dot{x}_1(t) \\ \vdots \\ \dot{x}_n(t) \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        The derivative <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)</m> is also called the
        <term>velocity vector</term>.
        Its length
        <me>
          \|\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)\|
          = \sqrt{\dot{x}_1(t)^2 + \cdots + \dot{x}_n(t)^2}
        </me>
        is called the <term>speed</term> at time <m>t</m>: it measures how
        fast a particle traversing the curve moves at the instant <m>t</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) \neq \mathbf{0}</m> for all <m>t \in I</m>,
        the curve is called <term>regular</term>.
        For a regular curve the tangent vector always points in a definite
        direction at every parameter value.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Example: tangent of a circle ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-tangent-circle">
    <title>Tangent Vector of a Circle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the tangent vector of the unit circle
        <m>\mathbf{x}\colon [0,2\pi] \to \R^2</m>,
        <m>t \mapsto (\cos t,\, \sin t)</m>,
        and verify that it is always orthogonal to the position vector.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Differentiating component-wise:
        <me>
          \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) = \begin{pmatrix} -\sin t \\ \cos t \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        To check orthogonality, compute the inner product with the position
        vector:
        <me>
          \langle \mathbf{x}(t),\, \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t) \rangle
          = \cos t \cdot (-\sin t) + \sin t \cdot \cos t = 0.
        </me>
        The tangent vector is indeed always perpendicular to the radius.
        The velocity is
        <m>\|\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)\| = \sqrt{\sin^2 t + \cos^2 t} = 1</m>,
        confirming that the unit circle is traversed at constant speed.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-tangent-circle">
        <caption>
          Position vector <m>\mathbf{x}(t)</m> and tangent vector
          <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)</m> at an arbitrary point on the unit circle.
          The tangent vector is always perpendicular to the radius.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-tangent-circle" width="48%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
              \draw[->,thick] (-1.4,0) -- (1.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
              \draw[->,thick] (0,-1.4) -- (0,1.4) node[above] {$y$};
              \draw[blue!70!black,thick] (0,0) circle (1);
              % angle 50 degrees
              \pgfmathsetmacro{\ang}{50}
              \pgfmathsetmacro{\cx}{cos(\ang)}
              \pgfmathsetmacro{\cy}{sin(\ang)}
              \pgfmathsetmacro{\tx}{-sin(\ang)}
              \pgfmathsetmacro{\ty}{cos(\ang)}
              % position vector
              \draw[->,thick,red!70!black]
                (0,0) -- (\cx,\cy) node[above right,font=\small] {$\mathbf{x}(t)$};
              % tangent vector
              \draw[->,thick,green!60!black]
                (\cx,\cy) -- +(\tx,\ty)
                node[above right,font=\small] {$\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)$};
              % right angle mark
              \coordinate (P) at (\cx,\cy);
              \coordinate (O) at (0,0);
              \coordinate (T) at ({\cx+0.12*\tx},{\cy+0.12*\ty});
              \draw[gray]
                ({\cx+0.10*\tx-0.10*\cx},{\cy+0.10*\ty-0.10*\cy}) --
                ({\cx+0.10*\tx},{\cy+0.10*\ty}) --
                ({\cx-0.10*\cx+0.00*\tx},{\cy-0.10*\cy+0.00*\ty});
              % angle arc
              \draw[->,gray!70] (0.35,0) arc (0:\ang:0.35)
                node[midway,right,font=\tiny] {$t$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Example: angle of intersection ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-angle-intersection">
    <title>Angle of Intersection of Two Curves</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The graphs of <m>f(x) = e^{x-1}</m> and <m>g(x) = x^2</m> intersect
        at <m>(1,1)</m>.  Determine the angle of intersection by parametrizing
        the graphs as curves and computing the angle between their tangent vectors
        at the intersection point.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Step 1: Parametrize both graphs as curves.</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{x}_1\colon \R \to \R^2, \quad
            t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} t \\ e^{t-1} \end{pmatrix};
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{x}_2\colon \R \to \R^2, \quad
            t \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} t \\ t^2 \end{pmatrix}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Both curves pass through <m>(1,1)</m> at <m>t = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Step 2: Compute the tangent vectors at <m>t = 1</m>.</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(t) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ e^{t-1} \end{pmatrix}
            \;\Rightarrow\;
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(t) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2t \end{pmatrix}
            \;\Rightarrow\;
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(1) = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Step 3: Compute the angle between the tangent vectors.</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \cos\alpha
            = \frac{\langle \dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(1),\, \dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(1) \rangle}
                   {\|\dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(1)\|\cdot\|\dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(1)\|}
            = \frac{1 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot 2}{\sqrt{1^2+1^2}\cdot\sqrt{1^2+2^2}}
            = \frac{3}{\sqrt{2}\cdot\sqrt{5}}
            = \frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore
        <me>
          \alpha = \arccos\!\left(\frac{3}{\sqrt{10}}\right) \approx 18.43^\circ.
        </me>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-angle-intersection">
        <caption>
          The graphs of <m>f(x)=e^{x-1}</m> and <m>g(x)=x^2</m> meet at
          <m>(1,1)</m>.  Their tangent vectors <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(1)=(1,1)</m>
          and <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(1)=(1,2)</m> form an angle of approximately
          <m>18.43^\circ</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-angle-intersection" width="55%">
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines=center,
                  xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
                  xmin=-0.3, xmax=2.5, ymin=-0.2, ymax=4.2,
                  xtick={1}, ytick={1},
                  width=6.5cm, height=6.5cm,
                  clip=false,
                  samples=80,
                ]
                % f(x) = e^{x-1}
                \addplot[blue!70!black, thick, domain=0:2.3] {exp(x-1)}
                  node[pos=0.9, above left, font=\small] {$f(x)=e^{x-1}$};
                % g(x) = x^2
                \addplot[red!70!black, thick, domain=0:2.0] {x^2}
                  node[pos=0.95, right, font=\small] {$g(x)=x^2$};
                % intersection point
                \addplot[mark=*, mark size=2pt, black] coordinates {(1,1)};
                % tangent vectors (scaled for visibility)
                \draw[->,green!60!black, very thick]
                  (axis cs:1,1) -- (axis cs:1.5,1.5)
                  node[above, font=\small] {$\dot{\mathbf{x}}_1$};
                \draw[->,orange!80!black, very thick]
                  (axis cs:1,1) -- (axis cs:1.5,2.0)
                  node[right, font=\small] {$\dot{\mathbf{x}}_2$};
                % angle arc annotation
                \node[font=\small] at (axis cs:1.45,1.6) {$\alpha$};
              \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Theorem: differentiation rules ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-differentiation-curves">
    <title>Differentiation Rules for Curves</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2\colon I \to \R^n</m>
        be differentiable curves, let <m>a\colon I \to \R</m> be a scalar
        function, and let <m>a_1, a_2 \in \R</m>.
        Then:
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Linearity:</em>
        <me>
          \frac{d}{dt}\!\big(a_1 \mathbf{x}_1(t) + a_2 \mathbf{x}_2(t)\big)
          = a_1\,\dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(t) + a_2\,\dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(t).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Product rule (inner product):</em>
        <me>
          \frac{d}{dt}\!\big\langle \mathbf{x}_1(t), \mathbf{x}_2(t) \big\rangle
          = \langle \dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(t), \mathbf{x}_2(t) \rangle
            + \langle \mathbf{x}_1(t), \dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(t) \rangle.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Product rule (scalar multiplication):</em>
        <me>
          \frac{d}{dt}\!\big(a(t)\,\mathbf{x}(t)\big)
          = \dot{a}(t)\,\mathbf{x}(t) + a(t)\,\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Product rule (cross product, only for <m>n = 3</m>):</em>
        <me>
          \frac{d}{dt}\!\big(\mathbf{x}_1(t) \times \mathbf{x}_2(t)\big)
          = \dot{\mathbf{x}}_1(t) \times \mathbf{x}_2(t)
            + \mathbf{x}_1(t) \times \dot{\mathbf{x}}_2(t).
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        All rules follow from the component-wise definition of
        <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)</m> and the corresponding single-variable
        differentiation rules applied component by component.
        The cross-product rule additionally uses the bilinearity and
        antisymmetry of the cross product.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-curves-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-curves-unit-circle-speed">
      <title>Speed of the Unit Circle</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}(t)=\begin{pmatrix}\cos t\\ \sin t\end{pmatrix}.
          </me>
          What is the speed <m>\|\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)\|</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>0</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The point is moving along the circle.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>1</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. Since <m>\dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)=(-\sin t,\cos t)</m>, its norm is always <m>1</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\sqrt{2}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The two squared components add to <m>1</m>, not <m>2</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The speed is constant here.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-curves-tangent-vector-parabola">
      <title>Tangent Vector of a Parametrized Curve</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}(t)=\begin{pmatrix}t\\ t^2\end{pmatrix},
          </me>
          compute the tangent vector at <m>t=1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Differentiate componentwise:
          <me>
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}(t)=\begin{pmatrix}1\\ 2t\end{pmatrix}.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            \dot{\mathbf{x}}(1)=\begin{pmatrix}1\\ 2\end{pmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
